---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import {CHANNEL_URL} from '../lib/channel';
import {CONTACT_EMAIL} from '../lib/support';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

/*
  No rate card here on purpose.

  Quoting a price before there are audience numbers to justify it either
  underprices the slot permanently or gets ignored. Ask for the conversation
  instead, and quote once analytics can back a number up.
*/
const FORMATS = [
  {
    name: 'In-video mention',
    body:
      'A short read at the top or in the middle of an episode, in the same voice as the rest of it. No jarring hand-off, no shouting.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Description and pinned comment',
    body:
      'A link and a line in the description of an episode, plus the pinned comment, which is the part people actually click.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Article placement',
    body:
      'A clearly marked sponsor block in the written version of an episode on this site. Written articles keep earning long after a video stops being recommended.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Repository sponsorship',
    body:
      'Every episode ships with a repo engineers clone and run. A tasteful mention in the README reaches people at the moment they are actually building something.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Sponsor - Code with Sam"
  description="Sponsorship and advertising on Code with Sam: formats, audience and how to get in touch."
>
  <h1>Sponsor Code with Sam</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Code with Sam makes short, technically precise explainers for working
    software engineers, and ships runnable code with every one. If you are
    selling developer tooling, infrastructure, or anything an engineer chooses
    rather than an executive, this is the audience.
  </p>

  <h2>Who watches</h2>
  <p>
    Backend and platform engineers. People preparing for senior interviews,
    people debugging something in production, and people who want the version of
    an explanation that comes with a repository attached. Topics run across
    Kafka, Java, Spring, distributed systems, concurrency, monitoring and
    observability, and architecture.
  </p>
  <p>
    The channel launched in September 2026 and is early. That is stated plainly
    rather than dressed up: early sponsors get an honest price and an unusually
    engaged audience, and audience numbers are shared on request rather than
    implied.
  </p>

  <h2>What a placement looks like</h2>
  <div class="cards">
    {
      FORMATS.map((f) => (
        <div class="card">
          <h3>{f.name}</h3>
          <p>{f.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2>What I will not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Recommend a product I have not used.</li>
    <li>Hide that a placement is paid. Sponsored segments are labelled, in the video and in the article.</li>
    <li>Let a sponsor influence the technical content of an episode, including which trade-offs get mentioned.</li>
    <li>Run anything aimed at engineers' employers rather than at engineers.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That is not posturing. The entire value of this channel to a sponsor is that
    the audience believes what is said on it.
  </p>

  <h2>Get in touch</h2>
  <p>
    Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> with the
    product and what you are trying to achieve, and I will come back with
    formats and current numbers. You can see the work first on
    <a href={CHANNEL_URL} rel="noopener">YouTube</a> and in the
    <a href={`${base}/`}>articles</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
